PLACES=[
N('alberton-courthouse','Alberton Courthouse (Alberton Museum)','Alberton','pe','civic','standing',46.81239,-64.06831,'exact','open','drive','','1878',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey wooden courthouse built in 1878 by James Wiggins & Sons, one of six circuit courthouses the province put up after the County Courts Act of 1873. It had a court on the main floor and a gaol in the basement; the only other courthouse built to this design, at Souris, is gone. It is a national historic site and houses the Alberton Museum.',
  'A steep gable roof, a round window in the upper gable, a flat-roofed entrance vestibule with an elaborate cornice, a row of single arched windows that keep their original glass, Eastern cedar siding on a brick foundation, and barred basement windows with hinged shutters where the cells were (Register). Interior not checked beyond its use as the museum.',
  'A courtroom upstairs and the cells in the cellar.',
  'Museum run by the community, 457 Church Street at Howlan Street. Tourism PEI lists the museum\'s dates of operation as June 1 to September 24; times not checked (museumspei.ca\'s page for it is gone).',
  [HP('Alberton Courthouse',1598),TP('Alberton Museum','https://www.tourismpei.com/search/OperatorDetails/op_id/4942')],
  brick='?',hours='June 1 to September 24 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('alberton-railway-station','Alberton Railway Station','Alberton','pe','rail','standing',46.8133,-64.0682,'site','public','walk','','1904–1905',
  'One of only two "boulder stations" on the Prince Edward Island Railway, built of granite stones in 1904–1905 to designs by the Charlottetown-born architect Charles B. Chappell; the other is at Kensington. It served the town until it closed in 1971. The rail bed beside it is now the Confederation Trail.',
  'Granite boulder walls, a hipped roof with its two chimneys, and the original doors and windows in their old places (Register). The station stands in Stone Station Park, a green space with picnic tables and an archway, and has been used as a tourist information centre (Register; whether it still is was not checked).',
  'A station built of boulders.',
  'Town park on Railway Street at the Confederation Trail; walk the park and the trail any time. Whether the building opens was not checked.',
  [HP('Alberton Railway Station',1747)],brick='no'),
N('yeo-house-green-park','Yeo House and Green Park Shipbuilding Museum','Port Hill','pe','landmark','standing',46.58754,-63.8918,'exact','open','drive','','1865',
  'A three-storey Gothic Revival country house built in 1865 for James Yeo Junior, son of the shipping magnate James Yeo, within sight and earshot of the family shipyard. The Yeos of Port Hill built or held shares in close to a fifth of all ships built on the Island. When shipbuilding collapsed the house became an oversized farmhouse; the province bought it and the land in the 1960s for a provincial park, and Green Park was made a museum of the Island\'s shipbuilding in the late 1960s and early 1970s.',
  'An octagonal cupola, deep verandahs, round-headed windows on the upper two floors, shutters and gingerbread bargeboards, on the site of the old Yeo shipyard (Register). Shown as part of the provincial museum; interior not checked.',
  'A shipbuilder\'s house that looks onto its own shipyard.',
  'Provincial museum (PEI Museum and Heritage Foundation) in Green Park Provincial Park, 334 Green Park Road. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 1 to August 29 and says to see the museum\'s website for exact dates and hours; that website (peimuseum.ca) refused this session, so times not checked.',
  [HP('Yeo House',1259),TP('Green Park Shipbuilding Museum & Yeo House','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/green-park-shipbuilding-museum-yeo-house'),W('Green Park Provincial Park')],
  th=[46.58924,-63.89056],thn='Lot off Green Park Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='June 1 to August 29 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('bideford-parsonage-museum','Bideford Parsonage Museum','Ellerslie-Bideford','pe','landmark','standing',46.61054,-63.91592,'exact','open','drive','','1878',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey Gothic Revival house built in 1878 by Thomas H. Pope, an accountant and telegraph operator, and sold to the Methodist Church in 1884 as its parsonage; it was the United Church manse from 1925 until 1972. Lucy Maud Montgomery boarded here with the Rev. Estey\'s family in 1894–95 while teaching at the Bideford school, and told the story of the "liniment cake" from this house in The Alpine Path. It was restored with a Millennium grant and opened as a museum in July 2000.',
  'An L-shaped house with a steep roof, a front wall dormer, Gothic finials, pendants and bargeboards, a bay window and a wrap-around verandah (Register); inside, rooms furnished as in the 1890s, including Montgomery\'s bedroom looking over the countryside (Register; the museum\'s own page).',
  'The window L. M. Montgomery looked out of in her first teaching year.',
  'Community museum (founded by the West County Historical Society), 784 Bideford Road, Route 166. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 4 to September 30; times not checked (the museum\'s own page gives none).',
  [HP('Bideford Parsonage Museum',13175),L('Bideford Parsonage Museum','https://bidefordparsonagemuseum.com/'),TP('Bideford Parsonage Museum','https://tourismpei.com/attractions/bideford-parsonage-museum-lm-montgomery-window-into-island-life')],
  brick='?',hours='June 4 to September 30 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('old-st-james-port-hill','Old St. James Anglican Church (the Old Shipbuilders\' Church)','Port Hill','pe','worship','standing',46.5673,-63.8788,'site','view','drive','','1841',
  'A small Georgian wooden church built in 1841 as a mission church, with James Yeo, the shipbuilder, giving 50 pounds; Bishop John Inglis consecrated it and its burial ground in June 1843. It was used until a new St. James was built across the road in 1885, and then as a Sunday school; its spire and bell came down in 1890. It was deconsecrated in October 2009 so that government money could repair it, and in 2016 it was described as a heritage and genealogy centre where the parish still held about one service a year.',
  'Never wired for electricity, it keeps its original box pews, fluted corner pilasters, eave returns, pointed Gothic windows with tracery, and a chancel window of coloured stained glass, on an Island sandstone foundation in the middle of its cemetery (Register). The old bell was hung again outside the door in 1988 (parish history). Interior seen only in the Register\'s photograph.',
  'Box pews and no electric light since 1841.',
  'Anglican Parish of Port Hill, Route 12, opposite the 1885 St. James. The cemetery is beside it. When the old church opens was not found: look from the churchyard. The parish\'s 2016 news item says it is run as a heritage and genealogy centre; current hours not checked.',
  [HP('Old St. James Anglican Church',8722),L('Anglican Parish of Port Hill: history','https://parishofporthill.blogspot.com/p/history.html'),L('PEI Heritage Buildings blog: 175th anniversary service (2016, quoting the Journal Pioneer)','https://peiheritagebuildings.blogspot.com/2016/07/parish-of-port-hill-celebrated-175th.html')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('notre-dame-mont-carmel','Église Notre-Dame-du-Mont-Carmel','Mont-Carmel','pe','worship','standing',46.39114,-64.033,'exact','view','drive','','1898',
  'A large brick Gothic church by the sea, built in 1898 as the third Roman Catholic church on this site since 1812 and the spiritual home of the Acadian families of the Mont-Carmel area. The parishioners raised the money and local craftsmen, some of them English-speaking and not of the parish, did the work; the estimated 450,000 bricks were made nearby at Frederick Strong\'s brickyard in Lower Bedeque. The architect was René P. Lemay, son of Pamphile Lemay, who translated Longfellow\'s "Evangeline" into French.',
  'A T-shaped church with two bell towers at the front corners, solid brick walls on a red Island sandstone foundation, traceried Gothic windows with stone lacework, rose windows and lancets, and a central door with a rose window and two carved heads (Register); the Register\'s photograph shows red brick walls and two grey spires. Interior not checked.',
  'Half a million Island bricks for an Acadian church by the shore.',
  'Active parish, 5785 Route 11. The diocese\'s Mass-times table (February 2026) lists Masses here, with a note "closed Christmas–May", but its day columns run together and the times could not be read with confidence. Look from the churchyard and road; the cemetery is beside it.',
  [HP('Eglise Notre Dame du Mont Carmel',1627),MASS],brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('cape-egmont-lighthouse','Cape Egmont Lighthouse','Cape Egmont','pe','light','standing',46.40185,-64.13406,'exact','view','drive','','1883–1884',
  'A three-storey square tapered wooden tower built in 1883–1884 by Laurent Perry for $1,695, to a Department of Marine and Fisheries design like those at Cape Bear and Wood Islands; Bruno Perry was the first keeper when it was lit in September 1884. The keeper\'s house and an oil shed of 1883 were removed in 1958, and the stone foundation was replaced with concrete in 1946. Because the cliff was eroding, the tower was moved inland in April 2000. It is the only light on the 46 miles of coast between West Point and Seacow Head.',
  'A white cedar-shingled square tower with a pedimented door, tall windows with pediment hoods, a balustraded platform and a lantern, on the point of the cape in the Évangéline region (Register).',
  'The one light on 46 miles of shore.',
  '161 Phare du Cap Egmont Road. Who owns it and whether visitors may walk to the tower were not checked; look from the road end.',
  [HP('Cape Egmont Lighthouse',19206)],brick='no'),
N('kensington-railway-station','Kensington Railway Station','Kensington','pe','rail','standing',46.43776,-63.63894,'exact','open','walk','','1905',
  'One of the two fieldstone "boulder stations" on the Island, built in 1905 by M. F. Schurman and Company to plans by C. B. Chappell, the third station in Kensington and one of the most elaborate in the province. Passenger service ended in 1969 and freight trains stopped in 1989; the line is now the Confederation Trail. It was made a national historic site in 1976, restored in 2007, and has served as a library, tourist office and railway museum; it now houses the Island Stone Pub.',
  'Fieldstone and wood walls with big boulders at the corners and round the openings, a steep gable roof with extensions on stone columns topped by copper finials, a track-side telegrapher\'s bay with a large arched window, and a front gable with Wallace stone voussoirs each cut with one letter of "KENSINGTON" under the date 1905 (Register). Inside it is a pub.',
  'A name spelled out one stone at a time.',
  'Private pub (Island Stone Pub), 62 Broadway Street North, beside the Confederation Trail. Tourism PEI lists it open January 1 to December 31; times not checked.',
  [HP('Kensington Railway Station',20380),TP('Island Stone Pub - Kensington Station','https://www.tourismpei.com/search/OperatorDetails/op_id/6469')],
  brick='no',hours='All year (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('keir-memorial-museum','Keir Memorial Museum (former Keir Memorial Presbyterian Church)','Malpeque','pe','worship','standing',46.529,-63.6866,'area','open','drive','','1927',
  'A plain wooden church built in 1927 by the Presbyterians of Malpeque who would not join the United Church of Canada in 1925, in an area where Presbyterian settlers had worshipped since 1810. It served as a church until 1983. Lucy Maud Montgomery, wife of the Rev. Ewan MacDonald, was among those who gave furnishings, such as the communion table. It is now the local museum of Malpeque history.',
  'A clapboard church 68 by 27 feet with an arched central door rising to a bell tower with a conical spire topped by a ball, false arches along the sides between sets of three windows, an unpainted interior of British Columbia fir, and an Island sandstone wall at the entrance to the grounds (Register).',
  'The church that would not unite.',
  'Community museum, 2214 Route 20. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation May 1 to September 4; times not checked.',
  [HP('Keir Memorial Museum',1775),TP('Keir Memorial Museum','https://tourismpei.com/attractions/keir-memorial-museum')],
  brick='no',hours='May 1 to September 4 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('north-cape-lighthouse','North Cape Lighthouse','North Cape','pe','light','standing',47.05773,-63.99695,'exact','view','drive','','1865–1866',
  'An octagonal tapered wooden tower built by the colonial government in 1865–1866 for just over $5,000, to plans by Alexander Anderson, at the Island\'s northernmost point, where a two-mile rocky reef runs out between the Gulf of St. Lawrence and the Northumberland Strait. Four storeys and 62 feet tall with three inner stairs, it has been moved back from the eroding shore six times. It was electrified in 1962 and automated in 1967; the keeper\'s house of 1866 is gone.',
  'A white shingled octagonal tower with a cornice, an observation deck with a railing and a red iron lantern, its windows in one vertical row (the ground-floor ones were removed) (Register). The Register says it is fenced off from the public for safety.',
  'The tip of the Island, where two seas meet over a reef.',
  'Route 12 at North Cape. The tower is fenced; look from outside the fence. Who manages the grounds today was not checked (Wikipedia: the Coast Guard maintains a light here, and a public nature trail runs nearby).',
  [HP('North Cape Lighthouse',19725),W('North Cape (Prince Edward Island)')],th=[47.0561,-63.99634],thn='Lot off Route 12 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('west-point-lighthouse','West Point Lighthouse','West Point','pe','light','standing',46.62029,-64.3868,'exact','open','drive','','1875–1876',
  'The tallest square tapered lighthouse on the Island, 67 feet 8 inches, begun in 1875 by Mugridge and Company of Shediac and the first lighthouse the federal government built on the Island after Confederation. Its first keeper, William "Lighthouse Willie" MacDonald, served from 1876 to 1925, and Bennie MacIsaac, the only other keeper, until automation in 1963. The keeper\'s quarters were pulled down in 1963 and rebuilt in 1983–1984 by the West Point Development Corporation; the site now has a museum and rooms for guests.',
  'A tower of cedar shingles painted in black and white bands (red and white until 1915), with tall windows under pediment hoods, brackets under the lantern platform, and a Wallace freestone foundation (Register).',
  'Sleep under a working light.',
  'West Point Lighthouse Inn and Museum, 364 Cedar Dunes Park Road, next to Cedar Dunes Provincial Park. Tourism PEI gives the museum\'s dates of operation as June 1 to October 10; times not checked.',
  [HP('West Point Lighthouse',18794),TP('West Point Lighthouse Museum','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/west-point-lighthouse-museum')],
  th=[46.61982,-64.38594],thn='Cedar Dunes Provincial Park lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Museum June 1 to October 10 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('st-simon-st-jude-tignish','St. Simon and St. Jude Church','Tignish','pe','worship','standing',46.95392,-64.03573,'exact','open','drive','','1859–1860',
  'A brick High Gothic parish church built in 1859–1860 to a design by Patrick Charles Keely, the Irish-born New York architect of nearly 600 Catholic churches, at the urging of the priest and later bishop Peter McIntyre. The young people of the parish gathered the lime and sand for the mortar of more than 500,000 bricks. Its stained glass came from Morgan and Brothers of New York, a Louis Mitchell pipe organ was installed by 1882, and in 1885 the Montreal artist F. Meloche painted the interior with religious scenes and life-size statues of the twelve apostles.',
  'A steep roof, pointed windows and doors, corner and side buttresses, walls of local brick, and a tall entrance tower with a spire and cross (Register); the Register\'s photograph shows red brick walls and a green spire. The Register says the painted interior, glass and organ still show "the artistic talents of an earlier age"; seen inside only at services.',
  'Keely\'s Gothic in Island brick at the western tip.',
  'Active parish, 208 Maple Street. The diocese\'s Mass-times table (February 2026) gives the Saturday vigil Mass at 4 p.m. in winter and 7 p.m. in summer; the other times run together in the table and were not read with confidence.',
  [HP('St. Simon and St. Jude Roman Catholic Church',1642),MASS],brick='yes',
  hours='Saturday vigil Mass 4 p.m. in winter, 7 p.m. in summer (diocese table, Feb. 2026); other times not checked'),
N('st-john-baptist-miscouche','St. John the Baptist Church','Miscouche','pe','worship','standing',46.43298,-63.86694,'exact','view','drive','','1890–1892',
  'A wooden High Victorian Gothic church designed by the Island architect George E. Baker and built by Schurman and Clark of Summerside; the cornerstone was laid on July 26, 1891 and it was dedicated on December 8, 1892. It replaced a plain church of 1823. The Register ties it to the pride Acadians took in their heritage after the Miscouche Convention of 1884, the second Acadian national congress, where the Acadian flag was adopted. Its bells were hung in 1901 and 1905.',
  'Twin square towers with spires on the front, a nave with side aisles and a clerestory, corner turrets at the east end, traceried Gothic windows with crocketed hood moulds, gingerbread bargeboards, and shingle and board-and-batten cladding (Register). Interior not checked.',
  'Built for the village where the Acadian flag was chosen.',
  'Active parish, 10 Lady Slipper Drive North. The diocese\'s table (February 2026) lists 10 a.m. Masses here but the days could not be read with confidence. Look from the road.',
  [HP('St. John the Baptist Roman Catholic Church',16622),MASS],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('oleary-railway-station',"O'Leary Railway Station","O'Leary",'pe','rail','standing',46.7074,-64.2249,'site','view','walk','','1913–1914',
  'A one-storey wooden station with a hipped roof, built in 1913–1914 to a standard Intercolonial Railway design: the third station on the spot where the Prince Edward Island Railway crossed the O\'Leary Road in about 1873, before there was a town there. A branch line to the West Point wharf was surveyed in 1911 but never built. Passenger trains ended in 1969 and freight in 1989; the town bought the station in 1983 and the rail bed became the Confederation Trail.',
  'Broad bracketed eaves over the platform, clapboard walls, double freight doors on the west side and the agent\'s bay on the east, and a First World War memorial of 1919 on the grounds (Register). It was turned into a café, shops and offices, with new windows and some steel doors in 2013 (Register).',
  'A railway town that grew around its station.',
  'Town-owned, 513 Main Street, beside the Confederation Trail. The trail and the memorial can be visited any time; who uses the building now and when it opens were not checked.',
  [HP("O'Leary Railway Station",20441)],brick='no'),
N('indian-head-lighthouse','Indian Head Lighthouse','Summerside','pe','light','standing',46.3798,-63.8172,'site','view','drive','','1881',
  'A rare octagonal wooden lighthouse with the keeper\'s room on the ground floor and a two-storey tower above, built in 1881 by the Department of Marine under the Island carpenter Thomas Beattie, at the entrance to Summerside harbour. It first stood on a circular pier in the sea; the long breakwater that now leads out to it was built in 1892. Keepers rowed out each evening and back each morning; none ever lived there.',
  'An eight-sided wooden house and tower with a red iron lantern and a railed observation deck, at the end of a long breakwater in Bedeque Bay (Register).',
  'A keeper\'s house in the sea that no keeper lived in.',
  'At the end of the breakwater off Route 112, Lower Bedeque; seen from the Summerside waterfront and the shore. Whether the breakwater may be walked and who owns the tower were not checked.',
  [HP('Indian Head Lighthouse',19730)],brick='no'),
N('seacow-head-lighthouse','Seacow Head Lighthouse','Fernwood','pe','light','standing',46.31592,-63.81012,'exact','view','drive','','1863',
  'One of the five lighthouses the colony built before Confederation, and the third after Point Prim and Panmure Island, put up in 1863 in heavy timber by David MacFarlane and John Rankin, perhaps to Alexander Anderson\'s design. It is one of the oldest wooden lighthouses left in the Maritimes. It guided fishers and later the ferry to New Brunswick, was automated in 1959 and moved back from the eroding shore twenty years later; the keeper\'s house of 1879 was removed about 1960.',
  'A 70-foot white shingled octagonal tower with a decorative cornice in place of brackets, pedimented windows and door, a railed deck and a red iron lantern; the Confederation Bridge is in view (Register). It was used in filming Road to Avonlea.',
  'A colonial light with the bridge in its view.',
  '198 Lighthouse Road, Fernwood, on a point over Seven Mile Bay. Who owns it and whether the tower opens were not checked; the Register calls it a scenic spot used by visitors and locals. Look from the grounds.',
  [HP('Seacow Head Lighthouse',19528)],brick='no'),
N('st-marys-church-indian-river',"St. Mary's Church, Indian River",'Indian River','pe','worship','standing',46.47676,-63.66942,'exact','open','drive','','1900–1902',
  'A large wooden French Gothic church by William Critchlow Harris, built from 1900 to 1902 after lightning burned the second church on August 4, 1896. Monsignor D. J. Gillis told Harris to build it "like Kinkora\'s church only bigger and better"; the parishioners hauled the stones and cut the timber, and Nathan MacFarlane built it for $20,000. The diocese closed it as a church in September 2009; the Indian River Festival bought it, restored it in 2010–2011, and uses it for concerts, for which its sound is famous.',
  'A round corner tower with niches holding figures of the twelve apostles, a lantern over the crossing, corner bartizans, alternating shingle patterns, drilled-hole bargeboards, many pointed Gothic windows, a Wallace stone foundation, and inside the original altar designed by Harris (Register).',
  'Harris\'s church that became a concert hall.',
  'Indian River Festival, 1374 Hamilton Road, Route 104. Go in for a concert of its summer series "Under the Spire"; dates and tickets on the festival\'s page (its box office was closed for the season when read).',
  [HP("St. Mary's Church, Indian River",18823),L('Indian River Festival','https://indianriverfestival.com/')],
  brick='no',hours='Summer concert series (Indian River Festival); dates on its page'),
N('lower-bedeque-school','L. M. Montgomery Lower Bedeque School','Lower Bedeque','pe','civic','standing',46.3434,-63.7659,'area','open','drive','','1886',
  'A one-room wooden schoolhouse built by the community in 1886 in place of a school of 1840, where Lucy Maud Montgomery taught in 1896–97. It closed in 1961 when schools were consolidated and decayed until the Friends of Lucy Maud Montgomery School began restoring it in 1989. It is a community museum; CBC reported in June 2019 that it would not open that summer for lack of help, and Tourism PEI now lists it with summer dates.',
  'Shingled walls, a gable roof with eave returns, a central chimney, a small front porch with a transom over the door, and paired four-over-four windows; inside, pupils\' desks, a central stove and a blackboard (Register).',
  'The schoolroom where Montgomery taught.',
  'Community museum, 14 New Road. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 21 to September 1, and "Open Fridays 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM before June 22"; other times not checked.',
  [HP('L.M. Montgomery Lower Bedeque School',3345),TP('Lucy Maud Montgomery Lower Bedeque School','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/lucy-maud-montgomery-lower-bedeque-school'),L('CBC News, June 24, 2019: school museum forced to close its doors','https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/prince-edward-island/1.5187652')],
  brick='no',hours='June 21 to September 1; Fridays 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. before June 22 (Tourism PEI)'),
N('ramsay-stone-house','Ramsay Stone House','Hamilton','pe','landmark','standing',46.4938,-63.7405,'area','view','drive','','1852–1860',
  'The only sandstone house left in Prince County, begun by Norman McLeod Ramsay perhaps as early as 1852 and nearly finished by 1855, near the first Ramsay homestead on Beech Point Farm. His grandfather John Ramsay came from Scotland on the Annabella in 1770. Norman was hurt working on the house and died in 1860; it passed to his brother and nephew and is still in the family, six generations on.',
  'Walls 24 inches thick of stone quarried about 600 yards away, lintels and sills each of a single stone, eave returns, a stringcourse on the east side and a door with transom and sidelights; the kitchen wing, a two-storey porch and the original interior were removed or redone in the 1970s and 1980s (Register).',
  'A Scots farmer\'s house in Island sandstone.',
  'Private home of the Ramsay family, 590 Beech Point Road. Look from the road only; the pin is the road at the farm lane.',
  [HP('Ramsay Stone House',18616)],brick='no'),
N('moase-blacksmith-shop','Moase Blacksmith Shop','New Annan','pe','industry','standing',46.4231,-63.6849,'area','view','drive','','1842',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey shingled forge built by Henry Moase in 1842, soon after he came from Devon, and worked by four generations of the family, last by Wendell Moase. It still holds many of the tools brought from England, and railway hammers, picks and bars that track crews broke on the line beside the farm, which Wendell Moase mended and kept as mementoes of the vanished railway. It was moved back from the road when Route 2 was widened.',
  'A steep gable roof, shingled walls and the old windows; inside, the forge and its original tools (Register).',
  'Four generations at one forge.',
  'Private, on the Moase family farm at 25578 Route 2. Look from the road; no visiting arrangement was found.',
  [HP('Moase Blacksmith Shop',1679)],brick='no'),
]
